 "upload": {
  "title": "Earth Just Spent a Full Week Above 2°C #shorts",
  "description": "Europe's Copernicus Climate Change Service says the global average temperature stayed more than 2°C above pre-industrial (1850–1900) levels for seven consecutive days, November 18–24, 2026 — the first such stretch on record. The threshold was reached on ten days this November; the data are preliminary. Copernicus climate lead Samantha Burgess said this is not a breach of the Paris Agreement's long-term limit, but called it \"a stark preview of where our climate is headed\". Scientists link the spike to a strong El Niño on top of long-term warming.\n\nSources: Copernicus Climate Change Service; The Guardian; Xinhua (citing dpa); WAM; science.ORF.at; MercoPress (based on search-result excerpts; some early reports counted six days). Earlier spells (2 days in Nov 2023, 4 days in Feb 2024) as reported. Map heat areas are illustrative, not data maps.\n\n#climate #weather #globalwarming #copernicus #elnino",
